WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.640
The San Andreas system accommodates relative motion between the Pacific and

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.800
North American plates. Its segments differ in creeping behavior, locking, and

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.480
earthquake history. The northern segment includes the 1906 rupture;

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.860
the Loma Prieta earthquake affected part of the broader system in

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.580
1989. Nearby faults can also produce damaging earthquakes.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.540
The central creeping segment moves gradually at the surface.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.020
Creep does not establish that every depth or adjacent fault is

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.500
incapable of earthquake rupture.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.780
Parkfield has been studied because of repeated moderate earthquakes.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.140
The southern segment associated with the 1857 Fort Tejon event is

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.460
an important example of long-term locking.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.460
Historical probability statements require their original date and time window.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.000
The textbook’s rolling “next 30 years” phrase is not a current

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.480
forecast simply because it appears on this page.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.060
A restraining bend in the southern system produces compression and thrusting.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.780
This helps explain why thrust-fault earthquakes can occur within a region

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.280
famous for strike-slip motion.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.020
Look for offset streams, linear valleys and ridges, scarps, ponds, and

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.760
drainages aligned with the fault.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.220
Match the aerial photograph with the named features in the source

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.100
illustration. Trenches and displaced rock units can reveal movement over different

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.300
timescales. An abandoned stream channel can preserve an earlier alignment.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.560
The East California Shear Zone contains related active faults east of

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.420
the San Andreas. Hazard assessment must include the network, not just

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.700
the best-known named fault.
